16
00:01:22,560 --> 00:01:24,950
Yeah, sure.

17
00:01:24,950 --> 00:01:28,230
So, geographic information system, so shortand would be GIS.

18
00:01:28,230 --> 00:01:36,310
It's basically a system that's designed to capture, store, manipulate, analyze, manage,

19
00:01:36,310 --> 00:01:40,550
and then present, I guess is a good word to say, all types of geographical data.

20
00:01:40,550 --> 00:01:47,990
So if you think of GIS as kind of a superpowered map that can do a lot more than just show you where things are.

21
00:01:47,990 --> 00:01:50,389
So it's very akin to any other kind of information system.

22
00:01:50,399 --> 00:02:01,671
Combines hardware and software data methods and of course there have to be the people that operate that system and interpret the results that come out of it. So that's kind of GIS in a nutshell.

23
00:02:01,680 --> 00:02:05,990
Many of our listeners may be familiar with relational data.

24
00:02:05,990 --> 00:02:11,110
Can you explain how geospatial data might be different to or similar to relational data?

25
00:02:11,120 --> 00:02:16,150
Yeah, I mean in a lot of ways it's very similar.

26
00:02:16,150 --> 00:02:20,470
It can be stored in databases, queried, linked just like relational data.

27
00:02:20,470 --> 00:02:23,030
In fact, we can store it very similarly.

28
00:02:23,030 --> 00:02:30,229
For example, let's say in a relational database, you might have a table of customers with their names and addresses.

29
00:02:30,229 --> 00:02:35,270
In GIS, you could actually link that address to a specific point on the map.

30
00:02:35,270 --> 00:02:37,990
So a specific place in the world.

31
00:02:37,990 --> 00:02:42,550
The biggest difference I think is that geospatial data obviously has a spatial component.

32
00:02:42,550 --> 00:02:53,110
Meaning that because it's tied to a specific location on the earth's surface, most relational databases don't out of the box understand that aspect.

33
00:02:53,110 --> 00:02:55,030
And so when you're talking about geospatial data,

34
00:02:55,040 --> 00:03:07,190
it's really built to not just understand the relationships that you would have through like a linked table, but also the spatial relationships between records like the distance between things,

35
00:03:07,190 --> 00:03:11,670
the proximity between different features, how they might overlap or connect.

36
00:03:11,670 --> 00:03:19,670
So following on that example that I gave earlier, if you have customers in New York City, that's something that's queryable in a relational data table.

37
00:03:19,670 --> 00:03:34,309
But in GIS, you could also take that steps further and think about how far those customers are from perhaps a new store that you might build or where those customers might live within a certain flood zone or any other contextual location information.

38
00:03:34,319 --> 00:03:37,990
Thanks. That was really helpful, Jennings.

39
00:03:37,990 --> 00:03:40,470
A moment ago, I heard Amy talk about the concept of a point.

40
00:03:40,470 --> 00:03:49,509
And when I was learning more about geospatial information systems, I learned that things like points and lines and polygons are all critical to GIS.

41
00:03:49,509 --> 00:03:54,309
Can you tell us a little bit more about what points and lines and polygons are?

42
00:03:54,319 --> 00:04:01,190
Yeah, so points, lines, and polygons are the primitive data types for GIS systems.

43
00:04:01,190 --> 00:04:08,309
And when you think about modeling the real world, you can imagine a point as say like a point of interest to which you're attaching data.

44
00:04:08,309 --> 00:04:13,670
So that could be a customer address and you might have other information about that point.

45
00:04:13,670 --> 00:04:23,590
But then fundamentally that point is going to have a latitude and a longitude when we're talking about geographic coordinates and that's going to represent an actual point on the surface of the earth.

46
00:04:23,590 --> 00:04:27,670
And if we string a bunch of points together, we can create a line string.

47
00:04:27,670 --> 00:04:32,710
And you can imagine a road would be best represented as a line string.

48
00:04:32,710 --> 00:04:37,430
And then if you have a line string that can close on itself, you can create a polygon.

49
00:04:37,430 --> 00:04:41,189
And that's going to be the best way to represent something like an area.

50
00:04:41,189 --> 00:04:47,189
And so we can put all these pieces together and you might have a polygon that's representing a city park for example.

51
00:04:47,189 --> 00:04:50,310
And so on a map you're going to show that as some sort of green space.

52
00:04:50,310 --> 00:04:54,469
And then you're going to have your line strings around that would be your roads most likely.

53
00:04:54,469 --> 00:05:01,510
And then you could have points within that park denoting points of interest such as a fountain or an information kiosk.

54
00:05:01,510 --> 00:05:09,189
And so you now have this inherent spatial relationship between each of these entities where in this database they might each just be these rows,

55
00:05:09,189 --> 00:05:13,510
but because they have these coordinates attached to their geometries,

56
00:05:13,510 --> 00:05:22,870
you're able to do these further operations as Amy was describing where you could query for, oh, okay, what are the points that are actually within this polygon representing the park?

57
00:05:22,870 --> 00:05:25,029
And you can create these spatial relationships.

58
00:05:25,029 --> 00:05:32,150
And another way to think about that is it's another form of join on a relational database, but you're getting a different wear clause.

59
00:05:32,150 --> 00:05:36,390
Right now you're joining on a condition that is using those geometries.

60
00:05:36,390 --> 00:05:41,510
And that's fundamentally different from say joining on an ID or another type of key.

61
00:05:41,510 --> 00:05:46,870
Okay, as I mentioned a moment ago, we're going to be talking about something called overture maps.

62
00:05:46,870 --> 00:05:54,230
And the documentation for overture says that overture provides free and open map data and that it's normalized to one schema.

63
00:05:54,230 --> 00:06:04,070
So I'd like to talk a little bit at a high level about what overture map provides and then additionally could one of you explain what it means when we talk about the overture maps schema?

64
00:06:04,080 --> 00:06:06,309
Yeah, sure.

65
00:06:06,309 --> 00:06:08,309
So schema, one of my favorite topics.

66
00:06:08,309 --> 00:06:10,230
I'm obviously the life of the party.

67
00:06:10,230 --> 00:06:20,870
So when you're thinking about schema, whether you're talking about traditional databases that are non- geospatial or if you're talking about geospatial databases, the schema typically defines the structure of the data.

68
00:06:20,870 --> 00:06:29,270
And so it's kind of like the blueprint for rules that dictate anything from what types of data can be stored to the names of the fields or the columns.

69
00:06:29,270 --> 00:06:33,350
And then of course the relationships between the different pieces of data.

70
00:06:33,350 --> 00:06:40,309
The key difference and Jennings touched on this a little bit is that the schema will also include definitions for the spatial data types.

71
00:06:40,319 --> 00:06:53,830
So where in a non geospatial schema you might have a field for address which is just represented as a string geospatial schema would actually include the geometry field.

72
00:06:53,830 --> 00:07:00,309
So like a point representing the coordinates of an actual location of that address and potentially other spatial attributes.

73
00:07:00,319 --> 00:07:01,990
Thanks that was really helpful.

74
00:07:01,990 --> 00:07:11,110
A moment ago you mentioned the idea of addresses and I think that connects to the six themes that are associated with overture maps data.

75
00:07:11,110 --> 00:07:16,550
The six themes are addresses, buildings, base, divisions, places, and transportation.

76
00:07:16,550 --> 00:07:21,189
Can the two of you help us out to understand more about what each of those data themes actually are?

77
00:07:21,199 --> 00:07:24,070
Sure thing.

78
00:07:24,070 --> 00:07:26,790
So, going back to that list, addresses is going to be it.

79
00:07:26,790 --> 00:07:35,350
There's about 300 million features around the world now in our addresses theme, mostly coming from open data sources, which is open addresses.

80
00:07:35,350 --> 00:07:39,589
And these are point features that represent actual addresses.

81
00:07:39,589 --> 00:07:47,510
So this is your street number, your house number, and the street name, and the geographical region for your location.

82
00:07:47,510 --> 00:07:50,390
And buildings is pretty self-explanatory.

83
00:07:50,390 --> 00:07:55,909
It's a data set now of about 2.4 billion buildings around the world.

84
00:07:55,909 --> 00:08:07,830
This is another combination of open data sources from Microsoft's machine learning buildings data set and Google open buildings and open street map buildings as well as EZRI community maps.

85
00:08:07,830 --> 00:08:14,869
And what we do is conflate all these data sets together and produce one unified layer of buildings across the entire planet.

86
00:08:14,869 --> 00:08:24,550
And so it's a very we like to think it's maybe one of the most complete open building data sets because we've been combining all of these other gigantic fairly complete open building data sets.

87
00:08:24,560 --> 00:08:28,550
And these are all going to be polygon representations of these buildings.

88
00:08:28,550 --> 00:08:33,670
And where we have information about the height, we also include that as one of the attributes on these buildings.

89
00:08:33,670 --> 00:08:38,870
And so you can get these nice extruded building models.

90
00:08:38,870 --> 00:08:41,589
And then base is a contextual layer.

91
00:08:41,589 --> 00:08:43,750
So base has types within it.

92
00:08:43,750 --> 00:08:47,110
And this is going to be your land use coming from Open Street Map for example.

93
00:08:47,110 --> 00:08:51,509
So these are your parks and kind of green spaces, water as well from Open Street Map.

94
00:08:51,509 --> 00:08:57,110
And then other natural features and infrastructure such as chairlifts and fences and other stuff.

95
00:08:57,120 --> 00:09:06,550
And so that's all coming from open street map and is converted into the overture maps schema so that it's compatible with the rest of the overture maps data set.

96
00:09:06,550 --> 00:09:12,230
And we like to think of this as going to be the rest of the color and detail kind of under the rest of your features on the map.

97
00:09:12,230 --> 00:09:23,430
And then we also include information in there for land cover as extracted from ESA satellite imagery and a bethimemetry layer as well so you can show some depth to the ocean.

98
00:09:23,430 --> 00:09:29,430
So yeah, that base theme covers everything you might want to add color and context if you're rendering a map from overure data.

99
00:09:29,440 --> 00:09:31,030
Thanks for that response.

100
00:09:31,030 --> 00:09:32,790
