00:09:36,470 --> 00:09:42,310
First of all, Amy, you mentioned that you've normalized all of the data to a single schema which describes the structure of the data.

103
00:09:42,310 --> 00:09:47,030
And then Jennings, you talked about how this data set comes from multiple sources.

104
00:09:47,030 --> 00:10:01,910
So the next thing that I'd like to talk about further is the challenges that you've experienced when it comes to unifying this geospatial data from different organizations into one normalized schema for data. Can you talk about that in greater detail?

105
00:10:01,920 --> 00:10:10,389
Well, maybe I'll start with just why that's important and then maybe Jennings can talk a little bit about some of the challenges.

106
00:10:10,389 --> 00:10:12,630
So imagine you're trying to build an application that uses map data.

107
00:10:12,640 --> 00:10:16,790
You're pulling all this information from different sources.

108
00:10:16,790 --> 00:10:18,710
if each source has its own unique way of describing things.

109
00:10:18,720 --> 00:10:33,509
So let's say some data refers to roads as quote unquote streets and others as highways or if you have in some data sets the height is measured in meters and others in feet just because of cultural differences.

110
00:10:33,509 --> 00:10:36,551
It's a total nightmare to get them to all work together, right? We've all been there.

111
00:10:36,560 --> 00:10:44,550
And so you spend forever writing this custom code and these translators to convert the data just to get it to a point where it's usable.

112
00:10:44,550 --> 00:10:47,269
So that's where normalizing to a single schema comes in.

113
00:10:47,279 --> 00:10:52,389
And it's like we all agree on a common language, a common structure for the data.

114
00:10:52,389 --> 00:10:54,870
And that's really key for interoperability.

115
00:10:54,870 --> 00:11:02,150
So when you have data that adheres to that single schema, it's easy to combine, it's easy to compare, it's easy to use from different sources.

116
00:11:02,160 --> 00:11:14,230
So for the overture releases, what we're doing is we're kind of doing that work up front to do a lot of that translation as we're bringing all these different data sets together so that developers that are using the data can just plug and play.

117
00:11:14,240 --> 00:11:16,230
Jennings, did you want to add to that further?

118
00:11:16,240 --> 00:11:19,829
I think that was a fantastic description.

119
00:11:19,829 --> 00:11:33,030
One thing that I think Overture is doing, Amy mentioned all of these custom scripts to normalize the data that people get stuck doing and unfortunately there's no way around that.

120
00:11:33,030 --> 00:11:40,630
Overture says well let's do it once in one place and then we will provide that unified schema and that's something that overture is trying to provide there then to the community.

121
00:11:40,630 --> 00:11:45,750
So we are doing a lot of those as you mentioned these complications and difficulties that we run into.

122
00:11:45,750 --> 00:11:52,230
We're doing a lot of this normalization ourselves as we bring in these different open data sets and then putting them into this schema.

123
00:11:52,230 --> 00:11:57,829
And so part of the idea here is just we'll do it so you don't have to to some level.

124
00:11:57,839 --> 00:11:59,750
Okay, that makes a lot of sense.

125
00:11:59,750 --> 00:12:03,990
When I was checking out the documentation for Overture,

