142
00:13:43,750 --> 00:13:49,750
If you could help our listeners to understand a little bit more about the scale and scope of overture, I think that would be helpful for us.

143
00:13:49,760 --> 00:13:56,949
Well, I think I would start with one caveat, which is we want to make this data as open as possible.

144
00:13:56,949 --> 00:14:02,470
So, by design, we don't necessarily put up gates for things like downloads.

145
00:14:02,470 --> 00:14:06,550
So, there are certain ways that we think about the data.

146
00:14:06,550 --> 00:14:17,990
One of the reasons why we decided to go with the cloudnative format, geoparet, and actually storing the data on the cloud is so that it would be easier for a variety of different applications to access the data.

147
00:14:17,990 --> 00:14:30,069
And just by default, in a lot of ways, we miss out on some of the what you would consider ways to track those metrics of who's downloading data or who is using the data, who is accessing the data.

148
00:14:30,069 --> 00:14:39,829
But I would say that the goal is really to not so much track those direct downloads or hits but understand how it's kind of cycling out into the ecosystem.

149
00:14:39,829 --> 00:14:47,030
So for example, Ezri uses Overure data as part of their living atlas.

150
00:14:47,030 --> 00:14:55,829
So if you can imagine hundreds of thousands, millions of users really that use EZRI software are able to access overure maps data.

151
00:14:55,839 --> 00:14:59,829
They might not know it's overure maps data.

152
00:14:59,829 --> 00:15:01,750
There's no attribution clause, so they might not even know.

153
00:15:01,750 --> 00:15:11,750
But the reality is is that they might be using it and it's probably percolating out to, like I said, millions, if not hundreds of millions of users just through one specific platform.

154
00:15:11,760 --> 00:15:13,750
Okay, that makes sense.

