00:24:34,950 --> 00:24:43,110
which allows us to first define an entity across our themes and then create this shared reference to it.

246
00:24:43,110 --> 00:24:44,789
So the idea being let's take a building for example.

247
00:24:44,789 --> 00:24:46,630
Buildings are modeled in many ways.

248
00:24:46,630 --> 00:24:55,350
They might have many different attributes depending on the data set and there's going to be multiple data sets of buildings for any city

249
00:24:55,350 --> 00:25:07,909
for example from different departments to different yeah different organizations and the idea of gers is to say okay well there might be different representations of each of these buildings

250
00:25:07,909 --> 00:25:16,070
but each building is in fact its own building entity the buildingness of the building so to say so we will define a single entity for a building.

251
00:25:16,080 --> 00:25:18,549
Give that a unique identifier.

252
00:25:18,549 --> 00:25:25,510
We're using the standard UYU IDs, so 128 bit random IDs.

253
00:25:25,510 --> 00:25:36,549
And overture then says, "Okay, as long as overture has any representation of this building across any data set, we are going to continue to use this ID."

254
00:25:36,549 --> 00:25:50,390
And we're going to do conflation on incoming data sets to compare them against our current understanding of this entity to see if a new incoming building matches spatially overlaps with some tolerance this existing building.

